<http://data.silknow.org/event/dbf923f6-dbc5-363a-88c2-ee5052e813a1>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Brocade silk is obtained by the interweaving of coloured silk and gold or silver threads.  Silk is described as a fibrous substance produced by silk worms in the form of a cocoon or covering within which the creature is enclosed and protected during the period of its transformation. The silk is cultivated and woven into textiles on a loom. Vegetable dyes were used to create color. Today, chemical dyes have gained popularity as they are cheaper, less time-consuming and produce a larger variety of colours.  Associated with ceremonial rites of ancient India, silk has been a highly revered fabric.  It continues to be a popular and widely used material."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/cd1723b8-b594-3069-b269-b71073c9427c> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Brocade silk is obtained by the interweaving of coloured silk and gold or silver threads.  Silk is described as a fibrous substance produced by silk worms in the form of a cocoon or covering within which the creature is enclosed and protected during the period of its transformation. The silk is cultivated and woven into textiles on a loom. Vegetable dyes were used to create color. Today, chemical dyes have gained popularity as they are cheaper, less time-consuming and produce a larger variety of colours.  Associated with ceremonial rites of ancient India, silk has been a highly revered fabric.  It continues to be a popular and widely used material."@en .

